	doi = {10.1007/s10798-025-10034-x},
	abstract = {Creativity in art and design education thrives on interplay among students, educators, industry, and AI tools. This study evaluates how an AI-Generated Content (AIGC)-driven co-creation approach, framed by the Humans-Machine/Technologies-Universities-Enterprises (HMUE) Model, fosters both structured and emergent collaboration. In a credit-bearing workshop at G University (2023-24), 32 students, 8 faculty, 6 industry partners, and 68 visitors participated. Surveys (n=100+) revealed strong creativity (M=4.21/5) and interdisciplinary learning (M=4.6/5), with (M=3.92/5) and market relevance (M=4.0/5). Interviews and open-ended responses highlighted that prompt-reflection cycles, "Tech Clinics," ethical checkpoints, formal faculty critiques, cross-campus alliances, and peer mentoring together accelerated iteration, reinforced confidence, and grounded innovation in real-world contexts, fully addressing RQ1 (creative impacts) and RQ2 (audience perceptions). This study provides actionable insights for advancing integrative design education and a foundation for future research in technology-enhanced pedagogy.},
