	doi = {10.1080/1750399X.2025.2533606},
	abstract = {Interpreting assessment is central to interpreter education, generating evidence of students' progress and achievement to inform better learning and teaching. Advances in generative AI, especially large language models (LLMs), present possibilities for automating assessment. In this article, we report on three inter-linked studies exploring the use of LLMs in consecutive interpreting assessment. In Study 1, we initiated a methodological exploration to evaluate how two LLMs - ChatGPT and LLaMA 2 - compare with automated evaluation metrics like BLEU in assessing English-Chinese interpreting. In Study 2, we examined how ChatGPT-generated feedback compares with teacher feedback in the formative assessment of English-Chinese interpreting, based on a longitudinal pedagogical experiment. In Study 3, we investigated whether ChatGPT can generate psychometrically sound measurements for summative assessment, based on an authentic final exam dataset. Our research uncovers LLMs' potential to revolutionise traditional pedagogical roles, emphasising the transformative capacity of generative AI in modernising assessment practices in interpreting education.},
